# Paco Syntactic Grammar (EBNF)

> Status: **living normative reference.** This file is the normative syntactic
> reference; `docs/grammar/tokens.md` is the normative lexical reference. It
> covers every decision recorded in `docs/design/decisions/` (ADR 0001–0017).
> It is not frozen: open questions are tracked in `context.md`, and a change
> here requires an ADR first.

## EBNF Notation

| Form | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `Production = Expression ;` | Definition of a production rule |
| `a \| b` | Alternation — either `a` or `b` |
| `a b` | Concatenation — `a` followed by `b` |
| `[ a ]` | Option — 0 or 1 occurrence of `a` |
| `{ a }` | Repetition — 0 or more occurrences of `a` |
| `( a )` | Grouping |
| `"terminal"` | Literal terminal string |
| `(* comment *)` | Informal comment |

Terminals written in **CamelCase** (e.g., `Identifier`, `Literal`) are defined
in `docs/grammar/tokens.md`. All other terminals are quoted strings.

---

## 1. Program and Top-Level Items

Every source file has the same fixed shape: its module declaration, then its
imports, then its items. Nothing may precede the module declaration, and imports
may not be interleaved with items.

An item may be preceded by outer attributes and by `pub`; an outer attribute
applies to the item that immediately follows it.

```ebnf
Program = ModuleDecl { UseDecl } { AttributedItem } ;

(* module nn — must match the name of the directory that holds the file.
   Every .paco file in a directory belongs to the same module (ADR 0015). *)
ModuleDecl = "module" Identifier ";" ;

AttributedItem = { OuterAttribute } [ "pub" ] Item ;

Item = FunctionDecl
     | StructDecl
     | EnumDecl
     | TraitDecl
     | MethodsBlock
     | ConstDecl
     | TypeAliasDecl
     | ExternBlock ;

(* #[test]  #[derive(Display, Clone)]  #[allow(lint_code)]  #[repr(C)]
   `@` is not an attribute sigil; it means pattern binding only (`n @ 1..=9`).
   See ADR 0020. *)
OuterAttribute = "#" "[" Identifier [ "(" [ AttributeArgList ] ")" ] "]" ;

AttributeArgList = AttributeArg { "," AttributeArg } [ "," ] ;

AttributeArg = Literal | Path | Identifier | OuterAttribute ;

(* use std::io
   use example.com/team/json
   use example.com/team/json as json      — alias
   Imports bind the module as a whole; there is no selective import.
   Every call site stays qualified: json::parse(text) (ADR 0015). *)
UseDecl = "use" ModulePath [ "as" Identifier ] ";" ;

(* std::io          — standard library, "::" separated
   example.com/team/json — external module, URL-like, "/" separated *)
ModulePath = Path | DomainPath ;

DomainPath = Identifier { "." Identifier } "/" Identifier { "/" Identifier } ;
```

### Visibility

`pub` marks an item as exported from its module. Everything without `pub` is
private to the module and invisible outside it. There is no other visibility
level and no capitalisation rule — the marker is always the word `pub`.

`pub` on a `struct` or `enum` exports the *type*, not its contents: fields and
methods each need their own `pub`. This is what lets a module expose a type
whose representation stays private.

### Constants and type aliases

```ebnf
(* pub const EPS: f32 = 1e-6
   The type annotation is mandatory — a constant is API surface.
   The initialiser must be evaluable at compile time. *)
ConstDecl = "const" Identifier ":" Type "=" Expr ";" ;

(* type Weights = Map<string, Tensor<f32>>
   type Pair<T> = (T, T) *)
TypeAliasDecl = "type" Identifier [ GenericParams ] "=" Type ";" ;
```

A `const` has no address and no runtime initialisation: it is substituted at
each use site. Paco has **no mutable global state** — there is no `static mut`
and no module-level `var`. Shared mutable state lives behind `Arc<Mutex<T>>`
like any other shared value, so the concurrency model's compile-time race
freedom holds without exception (ADR 0016).

### Foreign function interface

```ebnf
(* extern "C" { fn cblas_sgemm(order: i32, m: i32, ...) -> f32; } *)
ExternBlock = "extern" StringLiteral "{" { { OuterAttribute } ExternFnDecl } "}" ;

ExternFnDecl = "fn" Identifier Signature ";" ;
```

Every function declared in an `extern` block is implicitly `unsafe fn`: the
compiler cannot check a foreign signature, so calling one requires an `unsafe`
block. Exporting in the other direction is a normal function definition carrying
an ABI string: `pub extern "C" fn paco_kernel(...) { ... }` (ADR 0017).

---

## 2. Declarations and Signatures

```ebnf
(* Covers: fn, iter fn, comptime fn, unsafe fn, extern "C" fn *)
FunctionDecl = [ "comptime" | "iter" ] [ "unsafe" ] [ "extern" StringLiteral ]
               "fn" Identifier [ GenericParams ] Signature BlockExpr ;

(* The body is mandatory. Paco has no forward declarations: a function without a
   body is only meaningful inside an `extern` block (ExternFnDecl) or a trait
   (TraitFunctionDecl), and both have their own production.

   Valid modifier combinations are exactly:
     fn  |  unsafe fn  |  extern "C" fn  |  unsafe extern "C" fn
     comptime fn  |  iter fn
   `comptime` composes with neither `unsafe` nor `extern`: compile-time
   evaluation is sandboxed and rejects foreign calls (architecture.md §14).
   `iter` composes with neither: a generator is a pure synchronous state
   machine. The parser accepts the shape; the checker rejects the rest. *)

Signature = "(" [ ParameterList ] ")" [ "->" Type ] ;

ParameterList = Receiver [ "," ]
              | Receiver "," Parameter { "," Parameter } [ "," ]
              | Parameter { "," Parameter } [ "," ] ;

(* self       → move (consumes the value)
   &self      → shared borrow (read-only, the common case)
   &mut self  → mutable borrow

   The borrow sigil goes BEFORE `self`, matching every other borrow position in
   the language (BorrowType, UnaryExpr) and matching Rust. See ADR 0020. *)
Receiver = [ "&" | "&mut" ] "self" ;

Parameter = Identifier ":" Type ;
```

### Struct and enum

```ebnf
StructDecl = "struct" Identifier [ GenericParams ] [ WhereClause ]
             "{" { StructMember } "}" ;

(* Methods and associated constants defined inside the struct block (ADR 0002).
   Each member carries its own visibility. *)
StructMember = { OuterAttribute } [ "pub" ] ( StructField | FunctionDecl | ConstDecl | TypeAliasDecl ) ;

(* The `,` after a field or variant is required unless it is the last member. *)
StructField = Identifier ":" Type [ "," ] ;

EnumDecl = "enum" Identifier [ GenericParams ] [ WhereClause ]
           "{" { EnumMember } "}" ;

(* Methods and associated constants may also be defined inside the enum block
   (ADR 0002). Variants are exported with the enum; they take no `pub`. *)
EnumMember = { OuterAttribute } ( EnumVariant | [ "pub" ] ( FunctionDecl | ConstDecl | TypeAliasDecl ) ) ;

EnumVariant = Identifier [ "(" [ VariantFieldList ] ")" ] [ "," ] ;

VariantFieldList = VariantField { "," VariantField } [ "," ] ;

(* Named: Circle(radius: f64)   Unnamed: Pair(f64, f64) *)
VariantField = [ Identifier ":" ] Type ;
```

### Trait and extension block

```ebnf
TraitDecl = "trait" Identifier [ GenericParams ] [ WhereClause ]
            "{" { TraitMember } "}" ;

TraitMember = { OuterAttribute } ( TraitFunctionDecl | ConstDecl | AssocTypeDecl ) ;

(* type Output;            — declared in the trait, supplied by the implementor
   type Output = int;      — with a default
   A type that satisfies the trait declares a matching `type` alias in its own
   block. Referred to as Self::Output inside the trait. *)
AssocTypeDecl = "type" Identifier [ ":" TraitBoundList ] [ "=" Type ] ";" ;

(* Trait methods may omit the body (abstract) or include a default body *)
TraitFunctionDecl = "fn" Identifier [ GenericParams ] Signature
                    ( BlockExpr | ";" ) ;

(* Extend a type defined in another module (ADR 0002).
   Example:  methods string { fn trim(&self) -> &string { ... } } *)
MethodsBlock = "methods" [ GenericParams ] Type [ WhereClause ]
               "{" { { OuterAttribute } [ "pub" ] ( FunctionDecl | ConstDecl | TypeAliasDecl ) } "}" ;
```

### Generics and bounds

```ebnf
GenericParams = "<" GenericParam { "," GenericParam } [ "," ] ">" ;

(* Type parameter with optional trait bound:  T  |  T: Display + Clone
   Const parameter (ADR 0023):                const N: int
   Trailing const parameter pack:             const D: int...
   Symbolic dimension parameter (ADR 0029):   dim B
   A pack is homogeneous, at most one per list, and must be the last
   parameter: struct Tensor<T, const D: int...>. `dim` is contextual: it
   starts a dimension parameter only when an identifier follows it. *)
GenericParam = Lifetime
             | Identifier [ ":" TraitBoundList ]
             | "const" Identifier ":" Type [ "..." ]
             | "dim" Identifier ;

WhereClause = "where" WhereBound { "," WhereBound } [ "," ] ;

WhereBound = ( Lifetime | Type ) ":" TraitBoundList ;

TraitBoundList = TraitBound { "+" TraitBound } ;

TraitBound = [ "?" ] ( Path | GenericType ) ;
```

---

## 3. Type System

```ebnf
Type = PrimitiveType
     | UnitType
     | BorrowType
     | RawPointerType
     | ArrayType
     | SliceType
     | TupleType
     | DynType
     | FnType
     | GenericType
     | Path ;          (* user-defined or imported type *)

PrimitiveType = "i8"  | "i16" | "i32" | "i64"
              | "u8"  | "u16" | "u32" | "u64"
              | "int" | "uint"
              | "f16" | "f32" | "f64" | "bf16"
              | "f8e4m3" | "f8e5m2"
              | "bool" | "char" | "string" | "byte" | "type" ;

(* &T   &mut T   &'a T   &'a mut T *)
BorrowType = "&" [ Lifetime [ "mut" ] ] Type | "&mut" Type ;

(* *const u8   *mut f32 — FFI only. A raw pointer carries no lifetime and no
   aliasing guarantee, so dereferencing one requires an `unsafe` block. *)
RawPointerType = ( "*const" | "*mut" ) Type ;

(* [Type; Size] — e.g. [int; 64] *)
ArrayType = "[" Type ";" IntegerLiteral "]" ;

(* []byte   []int   []f32 — an OWNED, fixed-length heap buffer.
   `[]T` owns its elements and frees them when it drops. It is a shrink-wrapped
   `Vec<T>`: no spare capacity, no growth.

   The VIEW is `&[]T` (shared) or `&mut []T` (mutable) — making `[]T` owned is
   what lets the ordinary borrow operator produce the view, instead of the
   language needing a second, separate slice-view type.

   Representation: `&[]T` is a fat pointer (data pointer + length). The compiler
   collapses what would otherwise be a pointer to a pointer, so indexing through
   a borrow costs one indirection, not two.

   Slicing a range yields a view: `&buf[0..n]` has type `&[]T` (ADR 0020). *)
SliceType = "[" "]" Type ;

(* () — the unit type. The type of a function that returns nothing, and the
   success type of a fallible function that produces no value: Result<(), Error>.
   A signature with no "->" returns unit implicitly, so `fn log(m: string)` and
   `fn log(m: string) -> ()` are the same function. *)
UnitType = "(" ")" ;

(* (int, string)   (f64, f64, f64)
   A tuple has two or more elements. There is no one-element tuple. *)
TupleType = "(" Type "," Type { "," Type } [ "," ] ")" ;

(* dyn Sink   dyn Iterator<Item = int> — dynamic dispatch, visible vtable cost *)
DynType = "dyn" ( Path | GenericType ) ;

(* fn(int, string) -> bool — function type for higher-order parameters *)
FnType = "fn" "(" [ TypeList ] ")" [ "->" Type ] ;

(* Vec<T>   Result<T, E>   Map<string, int> *)
GenericType = Path "<" TypeList ">" ;

TypeList = TypeOrBinding { "," TypeOrBinding } [ "," ] ;

TypeOrBinding = [ Identifier "=" ] Type | ConstArg ;

(* A const generic argument (ADR 0023): an integer constant expression over
   literals, const and `dim` parameters and dimension names, the `Dyn`
   marker for a dimension known only at run time, a whole const parameter
   pack passed on as `D...` (the only argument allowed in the pack's
   position when used), or an existential `?b` (ADR 0029), allowed only in
   a function's return type and in struct field types.
   Tensor<f32, Dyn, 768>   Tensor<f32, M * 2>   Tensor<T, D...>   Grid<f32, ?n> *)
ConstArg = "Dyn" | ConstExpr | Identifier "..." | "?" Identifier ;

ConstExpr = ConstTerm { ( "+" | "-" ) ConstTerm } ;

ConstTerm = ConstAtom { ( "*" | "/" | "%" ) ConstAtom } ;

ConstAtom = IntegerLiteral | Identifier | "(" ConstExpr ")" ;

(* Module-qualified paths:  std::io::Error   Option   Vec *)
Path = Identifier { "::" Identifier } ;
```

---

## 4. Statements

Every statement ends in `;`, as in Rust. Newlines are plain whitespace. Two
exceptions: the final expression of a block, written without `;`, is the
block's value; and an expression statement that ends in a block (`if`,
`match`, `while`, `for`, `loop`, `select`, `unsafe`, `comptime`, or a bare
block) needs no `;`. A block-like expression at the start of a statement
ends there — `if c { a } else { b } - 1` is two statements, not a
subtraction — unless it is followed by `.` or `?`. A missing `;` is
`PACO-E0115`.

```ebnf
Statement = LetStmt
          | ExprStmt
          | BlockLikeExpr [ ";" ]
          | ";" ;

BlockLikeExpr = BlockExpr | IfExpr | MatchExpr | WhileExpr | ForExpr
              | LoopExpr | SelectExpr | UnsafeExpr | ComptimeExpr ;

(* let x = 10;      let mut y: int = 20;
   let (a, b) = pair;   let Some(v) = opt; *)
LetStmt = "let" [ "mut" ] Pattern [ ":" Type ] [ "=" Expr ] ";" ;

ExprStmt = Expr ";" ;
```

---

## 5. Expressions and Control Flow

Operator precedence is encoded in the grammar hierarchy, from lowest (top) to
highest (bottom). Each level passes through to the next.

```ebnf
Expr = AssignmentExpr ;

(* Right-associative: x = y = z *)
AssignmentExpr = LogicalOrExpr
               [ ( "=" | "+=" | "-=" | "*=" | "/=" | "%=" ) Expr ] ;

LogicalOrExpr  = LogicalAndExpr  { "||"  LogicalAndExpr  } ;
LogicalAndExpr = ComparisonExpr  { "&&"  ComparisonExpr  } ;

(* Non-associative: a == b == c is a parse error *)
ComparisonExpr = BitwiseOrExpr
               [ ( "==" | "!=" | "<" | "<=" | ">" | ">=" ) BitwiseOrExpr ] ;

(* Bitwise operators bind TIGHTER than comparison, so `x & MASK == 0` parses as
   `(x & MASK) == 0`.

   This is deliberate and it is not C's order. In C, `&` binds looser than `==`,
   so the same expression means `x & (MASK == 0)` — a silent, decades-old source
   of bugs that Rust corrected. Paco follows Rust here: the reading that matches
   how the expression is written out loud is the one the parser produces. *)
BitwiseOrExpr  = BitwiseXorExpr  { "|"   BitwiseXorExpr  } ;
BitwiseXorExpr = BitwiseAndExpr  { "^"   BitwiseAndExpr  } ;
BitwiseAndExpr = ShiftExpr       { "&"   ShiftExpr       } ;

ShiftExpr      = RangeExpr       { ( "<<" | ">>" ) RangeExpr      } ;

(* Non-associative: 1..10..20 is a parse error *)
RangeExpr      = AdditiveExpr    [ ( ".." | "..=" ) AdditiveExpr  ] ;

AdditiveExpr       = MultiplicativeExpr { ( "+" | "-" ) MultiplicativeExpr } ;
MultiplicativeExpr = CastExpr           { ( "*" | "/" | "%" ) CastExpr     } ;

(* expr as Type — left-associative, higher precedence than arithmetic *)
CastExpr = UnaryExpr { "as" Type } ;

(* Prefix unary operators and borrow.
   Note: & here is borrow, not bitwise-and (which is infix in BitwiseAndExpr). *)
UnaryExpr = ( "-" | "!" | "~" | "&" [ "mut" ] | "&mut" | "*" ) UnaryExpr
          | SpawnExpr
          | PostfixExpr ;

(* spawn expr    spawn { ... }    let h = spawn risky() *)
SpawnExpr = "spawn" ( BlockExpr | UnaryExpr ) ;

(* Postfix operators: method call, field access, index, call, propagation *)
PostfixExpr = AtomExpr { PostfixOp } ;

PostfixOp =
    (* Generic method call: expr.collect<Vec<int>>()
       Note: parser disambiguates "<" from less-than by lookahead. *)
    "." Identifier "<" TypeList ">" "(" [ ArgList ] ")"
    (* Plain method call: expr.push(x) *)
  | "." Identifier "(" [ ArgList ] ")"
    (* Field access: point.x *)
  | "." Identifier
    (* Index. One expression or several: a[i], a[i, j], a[i, j, k].
       Desugars to the Index trait: a[i] is Index<int>, a[i, j] is
       Index<(int, int)>. NOT valid on strings (ADR 0010). *)
  | "[" Expr { "," Expr } "]"
    (* Free call: f(x, y) *)
  | "(" [ ArgList ] ")"
    (* Error/absence propagation: result?  option? *)
  | "?" ;

AtomExpr = Literal
         | ClosureExpr
         | StructInitExpr
         | PathExpr
         | TupleExpr
         | SliceExpr
         | BlockExpr
         | ControlFlowExpr ;

(* Path with optional explicit generic arguments:
   Vec::new()    Option::Some(1)    channel<int>(capacity: 8)

   There is no turbofish. Generic arguments are written the same way at a free
   call, a method call and a type: `channel<int>(...)`, `.collect<Vec<int>>()`,
   `Vec<T>`. One spelling everywhere.

   The cost is that `f < g > h` needs lookahead to disambiguate — see §8. That
   cost was already being paid for method calls, so paying it once and being
   consistent beats two spellings for one concept. *)
PathExpr = Path [ "<" TypeList ">" ] ;

(* Struct initializer: Config { host: "x", port: 8080 }
   Note: not allowed as the expression of `if`/`while`/`for` to avoid
   ambiguity with block expressions — resolved in the parser, not here. *)
StructInitExpr = Path "{" [ StructInitFieldList ] "}" ;

StructInitFieldList = StructInitField { "," StructInitField } [ "," ] ;

(* field: expr  — or shorthand  field  (when variable name matches field name) *)
StructInitField = Identifier [ ":" Expr ] ;

(* (a, b, c)    a tuple
   ()           the unit value
   (a + b)      grouping — one expression, no trailing comma

   One production covers all three, which is why `AtomExpr` has no separate
   "(" Expr ")" alternative: it would match the same input. The element count
   decides the meaning, exactly as it does for the type forms (§3). *)
TupleExpr = "(" [ ExprList ] ")" ;

(* [1, 2, 3]   []   [0; 64] — the last form fills 64 elements with 0 *)
SliceExpr = "[" [ ExprList ] "]"
          | "[" Expr ";" Expr "]" ;

ExprList = Expr { "," Expr } [ "," ] ;

ArgList = Arg { "," Arg } [ "," ] ;

(* Named argument:  capacity: 8    — or positional *)
Arg = [ Identifier ":" ] Expr ;

(* Closures: |x| x + 1   |x: int, y: int| { x + y }   || { ... } *)
ClosureExpr = "|" [ ClosureParamList ] "|" ( BlockExpr | Expr ) ;

ClosureParamList = ClosureParam { "," ClosureParam } [ "," ] ;

ClosureParam = Pattern [ ":" Type ] ;

(* Block: { stmts... [tail_expr] }
   The optional trailing expression is the block's value (no semicolon). *)
BlockExpr = "{" { Statement } [ Expr ] "}" ;
```

### Control flow

```ebnf
ControlFlowExpr = IfExpr
                | MatchExpr
                | WhileExpr
                | ForExpr
                | LoopExpr
                | SelectExpr
                | YieldExpr
                | ReturnExpr
                | BreakExpr
                | ContinueExpr
                | ComptimeExpr
                | UnsafeExpr ;

(* if cond { }   if cond { } else { }   if let pat = expr { } *)
IfExpr = "if" [ "let" Pattern "=" ] Expr BlockExpr
         [ "else" ( BlockExpr | IfExpr ) ] ;

(* Exhaustive: the compiler rejects non-exhaustive match arms *)
MatchExpr = "match" Expr "{" { MatchArm } "}" ;

(* The `,` after an arm is required unless the arm is the last one or its
   body is a BlockLikeExpr. *)
MatchArm = Pattern [ "if" Expr ] "=>" Expr [ "," ] ;

WhileExpr = "while" [ "let" Pattern "=" ] Expr BlockExpr ;

ForExpr = "for" Pattern "in" Expr BlockExpr ;

LoopExpr = "loop" BlockExpr ;

(* select {
       v = rx1.recv() => handle(v),
       timeout(Duration::seconds(1)) => log("timeout"),
   } *)
SelectExpr = "select" "{" { SelectArm } "}" ;

(* Binding form: identifier = channel_expr => handler
   Plain form:   timeout_expr => handler             *)
(* Same `,` rule as MatchArm. *)
SelectArm = [ Identifier "=" ] Expr "=>" Expr [ "," ]
          | "default" "=>" Expr [ "," ] ;

(* Only valid inside `iter fn` blocks — see ADR 0004 / spec §8 *)
YieldExpr = "yield" Expr ;

ReturnExpr = "return" [ Expr ] ;

(* break with an optional value (for loop-as-expression) *)
BreakExpr = "break" [ Expr ] ;

ContinueExpr = "continue" ;

(* comptime { ... }   or   comptime expr *)
ComptimeExpr = "comptime" ( BlockExpr | Expr ) ;

(* unsafe { ... } — the only place where a raw pointer may be dereferenced or
   an extern function called. The block is an expression and yields its last
   value, so `let x = unsafe { *p }` is the idiomatic form: the unsafe region
   stays as small as the operation that needs it (ADR 0017). *)
UnsafeExpr = "unsafe" BlockExpr ;
```

---

## 6. Patterns

Patterns appear in `match` arms, `let` bindings, `if let`, `while let`, and
closure/function parameters. They are matched top-to-bottom; the first
alternative that fits the syntactic context is used. Semantic disambiguation
(e.g., identifier-as-binding vs. unit enum variant) is done by the resolver.

```ebnf
Pattern = WildcardPattern
        | LiteralPattern
        | BindingPattern
        | ReferencePattern
        | TuplePattern
        | SlicePattern
        | RangePattern
        | NamedPattern ;
```

### Individual pattern forms

```ebnf
(* _ — matches anything and discards the value *)
WildcardPattern = "_" ;

(* -42   3.14   "hello"   true   'x' *)
LiteralPattern = [ "-" ] Literal ;

(* [mut] name @ sub_pattern — bind the matched value to `name`.
   Example:  n @ 1..=9 => ...   |   c @ Shape::Circle(_) => ...
   Parser tries this before NamedPattern when `@` follows the identifier. *)
BindingPattern = [ "mut" ] Identifier "@" Pattern ;

(* &pat   &mut pat — match through a reference *)
ReferencePattern = ( "&" [ "mut" ] | "&mut" ) Pattern ;

(* (a, b)   (x, _, z) *)
TuplePattern = "(" [ PatternList ] ")" ;

(* [head, tail @ ..]   [a, b, c] *)
SlicePattern = "[" [ PatternList ] "]" ;

(* 1..=9   'a'..='z'   ..=100 *)
RangePattern = [ LiteralPattern ] ( ".." | "..=" ) [ LiteralPattern ] ;

(* Covers four overlapping forms resolved by the resolver:
     - Bare identifier binding:     x          (no "::", no parens)
     - Mutable binding:             mut x
     - Unit variant:                Option::None
     - Tuple variant:               Some(x)   Shape::Circle(r)
     - Struct variant:              Point { x, y }
   When the path contains "::", it is always a type path, not a local binding. *)
NamedPattern = [ "mut" ] Path
               [ "(" [ PatternList ] ")"
               | "{" [ StructPatternFieldList ] [ ".." ] "}" ] ;

PatternList = Pattern { "," Pattern } [ "," ] ;

StructPatternFieldList = StructPatternField { "," StructPatternField } [ "," ] ;

(* field: pat  — or shorthand  field  (binds field name as variable) *)
StructPatternField = Identifier [ ":" Pattern ] ;
```

---

## 7. Lexical Terminals

The terminals below are defined in `docs/grammar/tokens.md`. This section
summarizes them for cross-reference only.

```ebnf
Identifier = (* [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*  — not a keyword *) ;

Lifetime   = (* "'" Identifier  — e.g. 'a  'static *) ;

Literal    = IntegerLiteral
           | FloatLiteral
           | StringLiteral
           | CharLiteral
           | BoolLiteral ;

IntegerLiteral = (* 42  1_000  0xFF  0b1010 *) ;
FloatLiteral   = (* 3.14  1.0e9 *) ;
StringLiteral  = (* "UTF-8 text" with \n \t \r \" \\ escapes *) ;
CharLiteral    = (* 'a'  '\n' *) ;
BoolLiteral    = "true" | "false" ;
```

---

## 8. Known Ambiguities and Parser Notes

The following ambiguities are real; they are resolved by the parser using
lookahead, not by restructuring the grammar:

| Ambiguity | Resolution |
|-----------|------------|
| `f < g > h` — comparison vs. generic call | If `f` names a known type/function with generic params, treat `<` as opening a type list; otherwise treat as less-than. |
| `expr.method<T>(args)` — generic method vs. comparison | After `.identifier`, if `<` is followed by a type-list then `)`, treat as generic call. |
| `Foo { ... }` — struct init vs. block | Struct initializers are disallowed as the direct expression of `if`/`while`/`for` conditions; a parenthesized form `(Foo { ... })` is used instead. |
| `Identifier` in pattern — binding vs. unit variant | Resolved by the name resolver: if the path resolves to a value in the type namespace, it is a variant; otherwise it is a new binding. |
| `{ }` — block expression vs. struct init | Struct init requires a preceding `Path`; a bare `{` always opens a block. |
| `&` — borrow vs. bitwise-and | Different productions: prefix `&` in `UnaryExpr` is a borrow, infix `&` in `BitwiseAndExpr` is bitwise-and. Position alone decides. |
| `*const T` / `*mut T` — raw pointer vs. multiplication | Only ambiguous in expression position, never in type position. `*` followed immediately by the keyword `const` or `mut` is a raw pointer type; `const` and `mut` are keywords, so they can never begin an operand. |
| `module` as the first item | `ModuleDecl` is mandatory and may appear only once, as the first item of a file. A `module` token anywhere else is an error, not an identifier. |
